, -0.0299835205078125, -0.0235595703125, 0.00457000732421875, 0.01141357421875, 0.01352691650390625, -0.00899505615234375, 0.0229034423828125, 0.046539306640625, 0.0211944580078125, 0.005229949951171875, 0.0011615753173828125, -0.0233154296875, 0.0296478271484375, 0.03857421875, -0.032318115234375, -0.00537109375, -0.05535888671875, -0.043792724609375, 0.00011372566223144531, 0.0025997161865234375, -0.003025054931640625, -0.00800323486328125, 0.00814056396484375, -0.0135040283203125, 0.006084442138671875, 0.002227783203125, -0.016632080078125, -0.02398681640625, 0.0169830322265625, 0.0115814208984375, 0.01030731201171875, -0.0079345703125, 0.005764007568359375, -0.0170745849609375, 0.007373809814453125, 0.00299072265625, -0.000965118408203125, 0.0362548828125, 0.005229949951171875, 0.005252838134765625, -0.0256195068359375, 0.00455474853515625, 0.0165863037109375, -0.0015354156494140625, 0.00444793701171875, 0.00777435302734375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s nature, the vendor, and the invoice text. While AI is increasingly used to automate invoice categorisation, in-house small language models (SLMs) provide the dual advantages of lower costs and improved data security, confidentiality, and interpretability.\n\nIn this study, we assess this approach by analysing the pre-trained embedding geometry of a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa). We observe that the sentence-embedding space for this financial corpus is globally anisotropic but contains locally isotropic clusters\u2014extending previous token-level findings to sentence embeddings in a financial context. Notably, these clusters are strongly associated with vendor identity.\n\nWhen fine-tuned on a single GPU, SBERT achieves 0.96 accuracy in invoice classification, outperforming both a zero-shot large language model (LLM) and a vendor identity baseline. This improvement is particularly significant for smaller, more challenging categories and for new clients. For the critical generalisation task, SBERT attains an F1 score of 0.9 with approximately 100 client-specific invoices, demonstrating that in-house SLM implementations are highly effective.\n\nAdditionally, our geometric analysis shows that pre-trained embedding geometry correlates with classification performance. Interestingly, we find that providing structured input\u2014while helpful for human readers\u2014does not improve SLM performance, highlighting a counterintuitive aspect of model behaviour."}}